00:12:55.477 --> 00:12:57.849
potentially launch a career after the GSB.

00:12:57.849 --> 00:12:59.419
&gt;&gt; Cool, how about you Sarah?

00:12:59.419 --> 00:13:02.825
&gt;&gt; Awesome, so I'm in the Healthcare Club,
and that's been really good.

00:13:02.825 --> 00:13:03.473
For example,

00:13:03.473 --> 00:13:06.990
just a couple of weeks ago we did this
awesome track through Silicone Valley.

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:11.764
We visited a couple of different
startups in Biotech, and also and

00:13:11.764 --> 00:13:16.980
it was really good just to have that first
hand experience touring their companies,

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.918
getting to talk to the CEOs,
the startups, and

00:13:19.918 --> 00:13:22.950
then other folks in was really awesome.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:25.111
It's also really helpful for job postings.

00:13:25.111 --> 00:13:30.689
So, the different clubs will often
share internship opportunities and

00:13:30.689 --> 00:13:34.010
connections within that industry.

00:13:34.010 --> 00:13:36.572
The Healthcare Club's definitely
been really great for that.

00:13:36.572 --> 00:13:41.202
I'm also co-leading
the Adam Smith Society, which is basically

00:13:41.202 --> 00:13:45.996
a club where we debate free market
solutions to different problems,

00:13:45.996 --> 00:13:50.212
whether it's social security,
or healthcare, or pros and

00:13:50.212 --> 00:13:55.007
cons of globalization, and
that's just kind of a fun club to think,

00:13:55.007 --> 00:13:59.555
kind of like take a step out of your
everyday life here at the GSB and

00:13:59.555 --> 00:14:02.872
think about these much larger issues,-
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:14:02.872 --> 00:14:06.264
&gt;&gt; And so that's been really
fun to think about that, and

00:14:06.264 --> 00:14:09.340
be involved with some
of those discussions.

00:14:09.340 --> 00:14:11.820
It's also tied into the Hoover Institute
quite a bit, and so

00:14:11.820 --> 00:14:13.340
we get some really great speakers.

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.307
&gt;&gt; That's really cool.

00:14:14.307 --> 00:14:15.600
I know stuff that I've done.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:20.540
I'm involved with GSB Pride, which is our
LGBT community organization here on campus

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:24.210
that's been a great source of support
in meeting a lot of great new friends.

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:25.508
I'm also involved with,

00:14:25.508 --> 00:14:29.816
this isn't technically an extracurricular,
but one of the kind of flagship programs

00:14:29.816 --> 00:14:32.834
that the GSB offers is called
the Arbuckle Fellows Program.

00:14:32.834 --> 00:14:37.542
And what that is, is in your second year
you have the opportunities as an MBA2,

00:14:37.542 --> 00:14:41.902
to lead what's called a squad of
six MBA1s, first-year students, and

00:14:41.902 --> 00:14:45.487
together in that squad,
in this very small intimate venue,

00:14:45.487 --> 00:14:49.707
you work through case studies, and
role plays, and discussions, and

00:14:49.707 --> 00:14:54.626
reflections to really find out what kind
of leader you want to be, and as the MBA2,

00:14:54.626 --> 00:14:57.427
you are essentially
owning that discussion.

00:14:57.427 --> 00:15:01.386
You are helping your squad members
discover what it is that they maybe

00:15:01.386 --> 00:15:05.687
didn't know about themselves,
helping them finding strengths that they

00:15:05.687 --> 00:15:10.328
didn't know they had, helping them work
on weakness that they are aware of, and

00:15:10.328 --> 00:15:14.968
I know my experience as an MBA1 in squad
was tremendously rewarding, and I'm so

00:15:14.968 --> 00:15:18.108
lucky that now I get the chance
to carry that forward and

00:15:18.108 --> 00:15:22.750
help kind of shape just a little piece of
the leadership development that MBAs get

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:24.642
over their two years at the GSB.

00:15:24.642 --> 00:15:25.194
&gt;&gt; That's very cool.

00:15:25.194 --> 00:15:27.140
&gt;&gt; So, it's something I'm really,
really looking forward to.

00:15:27.140 --> 00:15:29.303
&gt;&gt; How many Arbuckle Fellows are there?

00:15:29.303 --> 00:15:32.110
&gt;&gt; I think there are about 70 or
so Arbuckle Fellows.

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:33.421
It's a competitive program.

00:15:33.421 --> 00:15:37.433
You'll typically apply, just in
the beginning of your second quarter here,

00:15:37.433 --> 00:15:41.443
in the winter quarter, and you'll
interview, you have essays to write, and

00:15:41.443 --> 00:15:44.845
then ultimately they'll select,
and if you are picked up for it,

00:15:44.845 --> 00:15:47.626
there's an extensive training
that you go through.

00:15:47.626 --> 00:15:51.053
You take one of Stanford's signature
classes called Touchy Feely.

00:15:51.053 --> 00:15:53.240
There's a lot of information
out there on that.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:55.550
I took it this quarter, it was amazing.

00:15:55.550 --> 00:15:58.500
And then, when you're serving as
in Arbuckle fellow, it's not just

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:03.300
the meetings with the squad mates,
you're also having the chance to take two

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:08.350
full quarters worth of classes where you
talk about what's going on your squad.

00:16:08.350 --> 00:16:12.400
You have a one-on-one coaching session
multiple times per the quarter.

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:15.450
And you really dissect it
down to what techniques can I

00:16:15.450 --> 00:16:18.980
use that make me effective as a leader,
and what techniques can I

00:16:18.980 --> 00:16:23.930
explore in a safe space that really might
be more effective for me as I go forward,

00:16:23.930 --> 00:16:27.670
and kind of try and find what's a good
fit for me in the civilian world.

00:16:27.670 --> 00:16:28.370
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:16:28.370 --> 00:16:30.740
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I'm really,
really looking forward to it.

00:16:31.750 --> 00:16:36.390
A question that's come in about a little
more on the nose for military stuff is,

00:16:36.390 --> 00:16:39.150
do GSB students tend to
serve in the Reserves?

00:16:39.150 --> 00:16:43.810
Can any of you comment on
sort of the reserve mix, and

00:16:43.810 --> 00:16:46.040
if you were able to incorporate
that into your GSB experience?

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:48.940
&gt;&gt; Sure, I really thought
about joining the Reserves or

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:51.910
actively participating in the Reserves
when I left the Marines about a year ago.

00:16:51.910 --> 00:16:54.500
I think it's a very personal decision.

00:16:54.500 --> 00:16:57.480
Like I said, I have a family and

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.050
my decision making process went around
what I really wanted out of the Reserves,

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:04.750
and what the cost and
benefits of that experience would be.

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:08.040
And for me,
it just seemed like the opportunity cost

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:11.430
to participate on the reserve at least
at this time, would be too high for

00:17:11.430 --> 00:17:13.820
my family and for
my academic experience here.

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:16.990
Though, I will say that
there's a small minority of

00:17:16.990 --> 00:17:19.890
the veteran population here that is
actively participating in Reserves.

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:23.380
And there's plenty of opportunities to
do that, if that makes sense for you.

00:17:23.380 --> 00:17:26.160
So don't be worried about
coming to the GSB and

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:30.410
saying you won't be able to
participate in your service, you can.

00:17:30.410 --> 00:17:31.900
There's plenty of
opportunities to do that.

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.480
The question is, does that make sense for

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:38.210
you in your current situation while you
are here at the GSB for those two years.

00:17:38.210 --> 00:17:41.430
I may very well actively participate
in the reserve when I graduate.

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:44.600
&gt;&gt; That's a great point, and I think
a lot people find that they maybe start

00:17:44.600 --> 00:17:49.290
in the Reserves and over the initial
time at the GSB, discover that it's

00:17:49.290 --> 00:17:52.760
not something that is compatible with
what their priorities are at that point.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:54.810
And that's the beauty of
the Reserves in my view,

00:17:54.810 --> 00:17:57.346
is that you can always
transition out of act of duty.

00:17:57.346 --> 00:17:58.420
Start with the Reserves,

00:17:58.420 --> 00:18:02.460
and if it's not working at that point
in your life you can transition out.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:06.080
Maybe look to join back up later on if
that's something that appeals to you.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:09.670
And I know that's been the case with
some of our fellow veteran community

00:18:09.670 --> 00:18:11.060
members here at the GSB.

00:18:11.060 --> 00:18:12.310
&gt;&gt; One thing I'd want to add there Steve,

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:16.790
is the fact that as you transition just
make sure that, if this is appropriate for

00:18:16.790 --> 00:18:19.030
you, you apply for
your reserve commission.

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:19.883
So that you have it,

00:18:19.883 --> 00:18:22.860
should you want to go ahead and
pursue that during your time at the GSB.

00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:25.431
So make sure you do
the paperwork beforehand, so

00:18:25.431 --> 00:18:27.870
you don't close that door unnecessarily.

00:18:27.870 --> 00:18:31.310
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, it's much easier to leave
the second time than it is to try to get

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:33.530
back in after fully closing the door,
as you say.

00:18:33.530 --> 00:18:34.280
&gt;&gt; Right.
&gt;&gt; And

00:18:34.280 --> 00:18:38.740
I don't think we have any in our class who
are actively in the Reserves right now,

00:18:38.740 --> 00:18:39.600
at least veteran members.

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:43.430
But I know in classes before,
people from all

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:46.800
branches of the military have found units
around here that allow them to drill, and

00:18:46.800 --> 00:18:49.370
that was our GSB obligations
with their military service.

00:18:49.370 --> 00:18:50.580
So it can be done.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:54.205
It's a very personal decision that you
really just going to have to think long

00:18:54.205 --> 00:18:55.020
and hard about.

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.150
We talked about the Reserves, Sarah
you're on active duty how has it been for

00:18:58.150 --> 00:19:02.990
you on active duty balancing kind of
this civilian educational experience,

00:19:02.990 --> 00:19:05.920
with the fact that you know
you're going back to active duty.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:08.885
What do you think you'll take back to
active duty I think is a good question.

00:19:08.885 --> 00:19:09.793
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:19:09.793 --> 00:19:13.150
I think that kind of ties back
to why I came to Stanford.

00:19:13.150 --> 00:19:15.960
One of the reasons I came to
Stanford was for the ideas.

00:19:17.960 --> 00:19:22.340
Stanford and the Silicon Valley,
there's such a different way of thinking

00:19:23.350 --> 00:19:25.510
that's sort of related to tech and
entrepreneurship.

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:30.870
And I wanted to be exposed to those ideas,
and take it back to the RMA.

00:19:30.870 --> 00:19:31.810
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:19:31.810 --> 00:19:34.850
&gt;&gt; Getting back to the first
part of your question,

00:19:34.850 --> 00:19:37.750
what is it like being here on active duty?

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:40.500
I think some parts of the experience
are a little bit different for me,

00:19:40.500 --> 00:19:42.752
is that I don't have to,
I feel like I have more freedom.

00:19:42.752 --> 00:19:47.008
I know everyone was really thinking
about their summer internships,

00:19:47.008 --> 00:19:52.380
like this is going to make or break it for
my career, it's really super important.

00:19:52.380 --> 00:19:56.500
I know exactly where I'm going after the
GSB, so I think it took a lot of pressure

00:19:56.500 --> 00:20:02.136
off of me to just focus on
areas I wanted to explore.

00:20:02.136 --> 00:20:07.040
And so I've got to do these,
I'm doing an internship in

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:12.165
Paris in finance later on this summer, and
I'm also working on this med tech company.

00:20:12.165 --> 00:20:12.813
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:20:12.813 --> 00:20:15.726
it's been good to just sort
of explore the space, and

00:20:15.726 --> 00:20:19.223
that's something that I think I
have as an active duty officer,

00:20:19.223 --> 00:20:23.580
versus other people who are more
concerned about their post GSB work.

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:26.922
&gt;&gt; It's pretty cool that you get to take
advantage of not just knowing that you

00:20:26.922 --> 00:20:30.583
will return to active duty and hopefully
bring these ideas to transform the army,

00:20:30.583 --> 00:20:33.340
but that you have the space to
take advantage of all that the GSB

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:34.745
offers in your two years here.

00:20:34.745 --> 00:20:38.310
I know all our other active duty veterans
who are here at least in our class right

00:20:38.310 --> 00:20:41.728
now, have found a lot of the same success
that you have in that department.

00:20:41.728 --> 00:20:43.105
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.
&gt;&gt; So, that's awesome.

00:20:43.105 --> 00:20:46.215
We're going to move on now to the audience
questions that have been submitted,

00:20:46.215 --> 00:20:48.376
we'll get to as many of them as we can.

00:20:48.376 --> 00:20:52.800
First one, let's see.

00:20:52.800 --> 00:20:55.110
This is a great one, so two of you
mentioned that you had served for

00:20:55.110 --> 00:20:58.010
ten plus years one married with kids.

00:20:58.010 --> 00:21:01.850
Do you find yourself challenged in any
different way by being a little older than

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:05.010
the average student, or have any
general advice for someone applying for

00:21:05.010 --> 00:21:09.090
someone that is a little more removed
from their college experience?

00:21:09.090 --> 00:21:10.480
&gt;&gt; Okay, I'll jump on this grenade.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:11.420
&gt;&gt; You win.

00:21:11.420 --> 00:21:18.135
&gt;&gt; So, I would say that one, we have a
over-30 group here, which is very select.

00:21:18.135 --> 00:21:19.447
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You have to be over 30 years old

00:21:19.447 --> 00:21:20.010
to be part of this.

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:21.559
&gt;&gt; It's one of the more
elite groups on campus.

00:21:21.559 --> 00:21:23.070
&gt;&gt; Elite group at the GSB.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:25.660
And it is heavily influenced by veterans.

00:21:25.660 --> 00:21:30.550
In all seriousness, I think that
having a family definitely affects

00:21:30.550 --> 00:21:35.150
my perspective, and how I look at the GSB,
and what I'm going to do here.

00:21:35.150 --> 00:21:37.290
I've got two kids under the age of five,
and so

00:21:37.290 --> 00:21:41.030
tea time with my daughter, or
wrestling with her, or my son and

00:21:41.030 --> 00:21:43.850
taking them outside to play,
is a big priority for me.

00:21:43.850 --> 00:21:48.290
And that means that I give up
other things, like networking on

00:21:48.290 --> 00:21:52.860
certain occasions or
social event that might be going on.

00:21:52.860 --> 00:21:54.740
So, I think being older and

00:21:54.740 --> 00:21:58.280
having kids does affect the way you
look at the GSB experience, but it's not

00:21:58.280 --> 00:22:02.100
forced upon you, it's a decision and
a priority that I've made for my family.

00:22:02.100 --> 00:22:05.200
Being older also gives me
the added advantage of

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:08.280
just being able to see things
with a different perspective.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:09.480
Right?
And that doesn't come with age

00:22:09.480 --> 00:22:14.800
necessarily, but it does come typically
with being in the military for ten years.

00:22:14.800 --> 00:22:16.200
I think that has been an advantage.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:18.959
The thing that may be a little
bit of a disadvantage,

00:22:18.959 --> 00:22:22.662
if I'm being totally honest is,
maybe your experienced is differ but

00:22:22.662 --> 00:22:26.492
working in the government means that
there's a lot more bureaucracy and

00:22:26.492 --> 00:22:29.095
a lot more older technologies
that I have to use.

00:22:29.095 --> 00:22:33.839
And coming to the GSB where everybody's
really super tech savvy, means that you

00:22:33.839 --> 00:22:38.441
might have to like pick up to tech game,
on how to collaborate with other people

00:22:38.441 --> 00:22:42.993
and how to excel to it's full capacities
as an example, or even PowerPoint.

00:22:42.993 --> 00:22:43.830
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But

00:22:43.830 --> 00:22:47.260
none of those obstacles
are insurmountable.

00:22:47.260 --> 00:22:50.341
But there were some differences that I
experienced when I first got to the GSB.

00:22:50.341 --> 00:22:53.040
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I really second that.

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:54.720
So I'm from the Silicon Valley.

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:56.936
So I thought,
like the tech thing'll be easy.

00:22:56.936 --> 00:23:00.132
And coming here there was all these
different modes of communication,

00:23:00.132 --> 00:23:02.640
whether it was like Trello,
WhatsApp, GroupMe.

00:23:02.640 --> 00:23:04.090
Slack, I was like, what's Slack?

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:09.140
And so it took some getting used to,
but we had tons of classmates that

00:23:09.140 --> 00:23:12.510
are super helpful, really kind and
understand the stuff.

00:23:12.510 --> 00:23:17.080
So if you're a vet my advice would be,
just sit down with one of your classmates,

00:23:17.080 --> 00:23:20.100
and they'll kind of walk you through it.

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:24.880
To answer this question first, one of the
strengths I think a lot of veterans have

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:28.920
is that we've been in situations before,
where you're just kind of like thrown and

00:23:28.920 --> 00:23:30.370
it's sort of this baptism by fire.

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:31.779
You figure it out.

00:23:31.779 --> 00:23:35.618
And so we figure it out,
and we persevere, and

00:23:35.618 --> 00:23:41.440
it was a bit rough navigating all
of the jargon the first quarter.

00:23:41.440 --> 00:23:45.750
Actually, if I have my one piece of fun
advice, that I'm actually very serious

00:23:45.750 --> 00:23:48.347
about, is to watch to show
the Silicon Valley, have you seen it?

00:23:48.347 --> 00:23:50.021
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; It's the greatest, [LAUGH]

00:23:50.021 --> 00:23:52.009
&gt;&gt; So as soon as I watched that show,

00:23:52.009 --> 00:23:53.786
everything made sense to me.

00:23:53.786 --> 00:23:54.286
I was like.

00:23:55.490 --> 00:23:56.840
Yeah.

00:23:56.840 --> 00:24:00.380
Cap tables, term sheets,
SAS, the whole thing B2B,

00:24:00.380 --> 00:24:03.260
it was all just jargon before but
now I've got it all down.

00:24:03.260 --> 00:24:05.270
&gt;&gt; Clearly you're ready for
that internship in finance.

00:24:05.270 --> 00:24:06.600
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; One thing I will say,

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:08.980
just to add to that,
if you've been in the service for

00:24:08.980 --> 00:24:13.120
a decade or longer, you probably will
have been, if you're an officer or major,

00:24:13.120 --> 00:24:16.730
or close to being a major,
you gotta hang that

00:24:16.730 --> 00:24:21.040
at the door when you come to the GSB and
just get in the mix with everybody else.

00:24:21.040 --> 00:24:24.680
If you hold on to your past so
much in what rank you used to have, it

00:24:24.680 --> 00:24:29.100
might be an impediment for you to be able
to really get the most out as experience.

00:24:29.100 --> 00:24:32.440
Albeit that some of your classmates
might be 24, 25 but they're here for

00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:35.335
a reason, and
they've got a lot of value to add.

00:24:35.335 --> 00:24:39.080
So sit alongside them and they'll be
teaching you something in the evening and

00:24:39.080 --> 00:24:43.220
it would be a sad day if you left
this business school without having

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:47.060
rubbed some elbows with some 24 year
olds who really know what they're doing.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:47.630
&gt;&gt; Absolutely.

00:24:48.860 --> 00:24:50.670
&gt;&gt; Awesome thank you, guys.

00:24:50.670 --> 00:24:55.260
Here's a question that came in,
after the GI bill on

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:59.400
yellow ribbon program assistance, what
would be the estimated financial burden?

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:00.820
So I can address this one.

00:25:01.850 --> 00:25:05.466
There's a lot of depths of complexity to
this one, so baseline assumptions going in

00:25:05.466 --> 00:25:10.040
I'm at at 100% eligibility for
the Yellow Ribbon Program and GI Bill.

00:25:10.040 --> 00:25:13.480
If you don't know what that means, make
sure to take a look at the VA's website.

00:25:13.480 --> 00:25:14.410
It can be a little complicated,

00:25:14.410 --> 00:25:17.420
and if there are more
questions we can help you out.

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:22.290
But if you're at 100% eligibility as I am
and didn't receive any sort of outside

00:25:22.290 --> 00:25:27.250
financial aid or fellowships, the level
of assistance offered is the same for

00:25:27.250 --> 00:25:32.270
everybody at Stanford, if they're at
100% eligibility for the GI bill.

00:25:32.270 --> 00:25:33.790
What that entails is, for

00:25:33.790 --> 00:25:38.410
the 2018 academic year,
roughly 23k from the government.

00:25:38.410 --> 00:25:43.410
With Yellow Ribbon funds the way they are,
that's roughly another 39K on top of that.

00:25:43.410 --> 00:25:47.885
So right now you're looking at, I'm always
terrified of math in public, but over

00:25:47.885 --> 00:25:55.080
$60,000 that will be furnished to everyone
who is at 100% Yellow Ribbon eligibility.

00:25:55.080 --> 00:25:58.420
And with tuition last year in
the neighborhood of high 60s to low 70s,

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:02.080
it's a significant chunk
covered by Stanford for

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:03.600
people at full Yellow Ribbon eligibility.

00:26:03.600 --> 00:26:07.940
So very, very helpful for
veterans that have served for a long time.

00:26:07.940 --> 00:26:09.070
And then remember, on top of that,

00:26:09.070 --> 00:26:11.720
that there's also the housing
allowance that's paid to you.

00:26:11.720 --> 00:26:16.250
Every month that appears on the order
of 3,600 a month that's fixed to

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:17.310
cost of living for the area.

00:26:17.310 --> 00:26:20.810
So that's a huge benefit, especially
if you live out in town like me.

00:26:20.810 --> 00:26:26.050
And there's also a tuition textbook
stipend that's paid every year as well,

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:26.940
it's about $1,000.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:30.967
So bottom line, a significant amount of
financial aid is available to those who

00:26:30.967 --> 00:26:33.360
are at 100% Yellow Ribbon eligibility.

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:37.993
And even for those who aren't, there
are tons of options that the GI bell, or

00:26:37.993 --> 00:26:43.305
the GSB financial aid office can educate
you on that might be workable for you.

00:26:43.305 --> 00:26:49.110
There's standard federal loans, there's
private loans, there's another GI or

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:52.160
a VA program called Vocational Rehab
that I'm not super smart on.

00:26:52.160 --> 00:26:56.100
But the bottom line is there are a ton
of options out there for financial aid.

00:26:56.100 --> 00:26:58.380
And if you really want to
come to Stanford,

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:03.830
I would say that try to make that be just
one part of the overall calculation.

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:08.170
And it may have some more weight for
some as it does for others.

00:27:08.170 --> 00:27:13.320
But only you know that so just give it
the due consideration that it deserves,

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:16.650
regardless if you're at full eligibility,
I can tell you that the GI bill is very,

00:27:16.650 --> 00:27:19.635
very helpful, attending Stanford
certainly has helped me.

00:27:19.635 --> 00:27:21.150
&gt;&gt; I just add one more thing as well.

00:27:21.150 --> 00:27:26.620
As you consider what schools you want to
go to, once you get accepted to the many

00:27:26.620 --> 00:27:30.800
schools you probably want to apply to,
take an apples to apples comparison.

00:27:30.800 --> 00:27:33.660
And actually compare what you would get if

00:27:33.660 --> 00:27:37.594
you were to go to another school
because I was actually shocked as I

00:27:37.594 --> 00:27:41.697
did the comparison myself as we
transitioned as a family to the GSB,

00:27:41.697 --> 00:27:45.430
that the GSB after all the support
provided me was actually much more

00:27:45.430 --> 00:27:49.540
affordable than some other schools
that I expected to be more affordable.

00:27:49.540 --> 00:27:53.090
So it actually worked out to
be economically a line for

00:27:53.090 --> 00:27:55.120
me to come to the GSB, and
that was to my surprise.

00:27:55.120 --> 00:27:58.130
So just do the analysis,
practice your pre MBA skills,

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:02.690
make an Excel sheet, and
adjust for the cost of living, and

00:28:02.690 --> 00:28:06.360
you might be surprised that coming to
the GSB is actually the best place to go.

00:28:06.360 --> 00:28:07.350
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:28:07.350 --> 00:28:10.480
Got time for just a couple more questions
if we keep our answers pretty quick.

00:28:10.480 --> 00:28:11.510
So we'll just try to get to them here.

00:28:13.230 --> 00:28:16.700
Looking back, following your first year,
what would you recommend potential

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:19.540
students do to best prepare themselves
prior to returning to school?

00:28:21.250 --> 00:28:23.350
I've got two one-sentence answers.

00:28:23.350 --> 00:28:25.938
The first one would be try
to relax a little bit.

00:28:25.938 --> 00:28:28.377
You'll definitely,
if you've been picked to come here,

00:28:28.377 --> 00:28:29.708
you have the skills to succeed.

00:28:29.708 --> 00:28:34.309
So no need to super overload if you don't
want to if you feel comfortable kind of

00:28:34.309 --> 00:28:38.490
doing some prep on like Excel or
something like that, you can do that.

00:28:38.490 --> 00:28:43.170
I would say you don't need to and then
my other quick sentence advice would be

00:28:43.170 --> 00:28:47.070
look at the possibility and see if it's
right for you to do a pre MBA internship.

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:50.635
There are a lot of big companies and
tech and finance and

00:28:50.635 --> 00:28:55.120
consulting that offer veteran specific
opportunities to work in finance or

00:28:55.120 --> 00:28:57.770
work in consulting for a summer,
just to get a taste of it.

00:28:57.770 --> 00:29:01.880
And that's essentially a free internship
where you can either set yourself up to

00:29:01.880 --> 00:29:06.420
go back to that field if you really
like it and your first MBA 1 internship.

00:29:06.420 --> 00:29:09.880
Or if you don't like it, you've learned a
lot and you have an educated perspective,

00:29:09.880 --> 00:29:11.670
eyes open,
about what it means to work there.

00:29:11.670 --> 00:29:16.100
So that's kind of my advice that I
would give myself, looking back on it.

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:18.270
What do you all think?

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:21.860
&gt;&gt; So I'll just reiterate my first
piece of advice which is watch the show

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:22.540
Silicon Valley.

00:29:22.540 --> 00:29:23.527
because everything will make sense.

00:29:23.527 --> 00:29:24.208
&gt;&gt; Excellent, underline.

00:29:24.208 --> 00:29:25.910
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:29:25.910 --> 00:29:28.164
And, I totally second you on the Excel.

00:29:28.164 --> 00:29:32.101
If you have a chance, and you want to
take some of the more accelerated or

00:29:32.101 --> 00:29:36.441
the advanced classes when you get on to
campus, I would encourage students or

00:29:36.441 --> 00:29:40.022
admits to take a look at the study
materials that they give you.

00:29:40.022 --> 00:29:43.011
And try to brush up on some of these
subjects, especially if you want to place

00:29:43.011 --> 00:29:46.040
into some of these accelerator,
or higher level classes.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:48.530
That being said,
if you don't have the time,

00:29:48.530 --> 00:29:52.490
don't stress out about it
because you'll be fine.

00:29:52.490 --> 00:29:53.140
I didn't.

00:29:53.140 --> 00:29:55.710
I was really busy leading
up to coming here so

00:29:55.710 --> 00:29:59.880
I didn't have the time to work on
any of that stuff and I was fine.

00:29:59.880 --> 00:30:03.120
So just do whatever makes sense for you.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:04.350
&gt;&gt; Cool.

00:30:04.350 --> 00:30:04.940
&gt;&gt; Really quickly,

00:30:04.940 --> 00:30:08.400
I would say that you won't have a ton of
time here to do some deep level thinking

00:30:08.400 --> 00:30:12.490
about what you want your life to look like
after the GSB, which is a bit surprising.

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:14.620
So I would say if you've got the time and
the bandwidth,

00:30:14.620 --> 00:30:18.430
I would definitely just sit down and
talk to your significant other or

00:30:18.430 --> 00:30:22.210
your friends and like kind of design
your way through what you want

00:30:22.210 --> 00:30:25.660
your life to look like outside of the GSB
so you can hit the ground running.

00:30:25.660 --> 00:30:29.910
Even with the academic adjustment period
being what it is here, recruiting

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:33.270
starts pretty quickly and you won't have
the time to really think about it, so

00:30:33.270 --> 00:30:35.810
if you can think about it before you
come to the GSB, it'd be very helpful.

00:30:36.810 --> 00:30:39.780
&gt;&gt; And then, I think, maybe,
we have time for one or two more.

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:41.110
We've got the high sign.

00:30:41.110 --> 00:30:42.020
Perfect.

00:30:42.020 --> 00:30:45.300
All right, did you guys come to Stanford
GSB knowing exactly what field you wanted

00:30:45.300 --> 00:30:48.810
to specialize in, and how's your
perpsective changed since your time here?

00:30:48.810 --> 00:30:50.160
Do you want to start us out on that?

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:52.300
&gt;&gt; Sure, I did not know.

00:30:52.300 --> 00:30:56.311
I said that piece of advice because I
didn't have the time to think about what I

00:30:56.311 --> 00:30:56.814
wanted.

00:30:56.814 --> 00:31:00.928
And so, my perspective has certainly
changed partially because I know a lot

00:31:00.928 --> 00:31:05.318
more about what private equity looks like
now, and what investment banking is.

00:31:05.318 --> 00:31:08.391
I didn't know what any of those things
were really before I came here, and

00:31:08.391 --> 00:31:11.690
I realized some of those things
aren't just for me and my family.

00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:15.790
And so I think as you learn more about
each of these possibilities, you'll make

00:31:15.790 --> 00:31:19.575
the right decision based on your desires
and what you're really capable of doing.

00:31:19.575 --> 00:31:23.913
GSB opens a lot of doors, but there
are some things that you're just not going

00:31:23.913 --> 00:31:27.983
to be able to do because you spent
11 years or 10 years in the service,

00:31:27.983 --> 00:31:32.683
as compared to your colleagues who did
investment banking right out of undergrad.

00:31:32.683 --> 00:31:36.823
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah I would say that
veterans were all by the nature of our

00:31:36.823 --> 00:31:40.964
transition, we're transitioning
from one career field to another.

00:31:40.964 --> 00:31:44.146
So I think there's very much the
expectation that you don't know what you

00:31:44.146 --> 00:31:44.670
want to do.

00:31:44.670 --> 00:31:46.020
It's very common.

00:31:46.020 --> 00:31:48.210
I think it's definitely really
a good idea to have priorities or

00:31:48.210 --> 00:31:51.420
areas of interest before you come here so
you don't lose focus.

00:31:51.420 --> 00:31:54.320
But you'll figure it out
as you get exposed to sort

00:31:54.320 --> 00:31:56.978
of the smorgasbord of
different opportunities.

00:31:56.978 --> 00:32:01.260
That's an excellent word, that's
an MBA word right there, [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:02.448
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So

00:32:02.448 --> 00:32:08.280
I guess I also didn't know
exactly what I wanted to do.

00:32:08.280 --> 00:32:11.290
I was pretty focused in where I thought
I wanted to end up in aerospace,

00:32:11.290 --> 00:32:13.590
which is related to my background.

00:32:13.590 --> 00:32:15.210
That's I'd say probably not

00:32:16.310 --> 00:32:19.570
in my experience most veterans end up
doing something completely different than

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:23.420
what they did in the military when they
leave and go to the business world.

00:32:23.420 --> 00:32:25.730
I'm kind of tangentially
staying in the same way.

00:32:25.730 --> 00:32:27.290
So I knew where I wanted to end up, but

00:32:27.290 --> 00:32:30.860
as far as function, I had no idea and
frankly still don't.

00:32:30.860 --> 00:32:33.960
I had the opportunity to
do a pre-MBA internship for

00:32:33.960 --> 00:32:37.490
an aircraft leasing company that I'll
be returning to for half the summer.

00:32:37.490 --> 00:32:41.500
And then I'll also be working for
an aerospace startup as well for

00:32:41.500 --> 00:32:42.470
the second half of my summer.

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:45.890
So I'm exploring different functions
within one broader industry.

00:32:45.890 --> 00:32:50.590
And the GSB, far and away,
I would not have had the chance to

00:32:50.590 --> 00:32:54.130
explore either of these internships,
both of which are really exciting,

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:57.860
without the resources and the benefits
of being here in this program.

00:32:57.860 --> 00:33:02.020
So yeah, I didn't know what I wanted to
specialize in either and I still don't.

00:33:02.020 --> 00:33:03.870
Hopefully we'll figure
it out over the summer.

00:33:03.870 --> 00:33:05.520
But if you don't, it's totally cool,

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:07.650
because a lot of people have
that exact same experience.

00:33:07.650 --> 00:33:11.834
And there's a chance to reinvent yourself
ten different times at the GSP if you want

00:33:11.834 --> 00:33:12.929
to and if you need to.

00:33:12.929 --> 00:33:14.710
So certainly nothing to be afraid of.

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:19.560
What, this may be our last one.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:20.583
How about this?

00:33:20.583 --> 00:33:24.530
How do you think your military experience
helps strengthen your application?

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:28.277
And what other extracurricular
activities did you have that

00:33:28.277 --> 00:33:31.592
you believe maybe were an asset
to you as you applied?

00:33:31.592 --> 00:33:34.290
I'll start on that one.

00:33:34.290 --> 00:33:38.980
I think our military experience, for me,
it gave me a lot of, really, unique and

00:33:38.980 --> 00:33:41.470
interesting stories that helped show

00:33:41.470 --> 00:33:45.990
ways in which I was able to demonstrate
leadership in my work in the Navy.

00:33:45.990 --> 00:33:49.786
And that is such a central focus here
at the GSB, that I think your average

00:33:49.786 --> 00:33:53.290
military veteran often has a huge
bank of those stories to draw on.

00:33:53.290 --> 00:33:57.866
And I think that can really strengthen
your application, make it more powerful,

00:33:57.866 --> 00:34:01.030
make it about you and
show the various talents you have.

00:34:01.030 --> 00:34:05.570
So just the kind of unique experience
that as a military member you

00:34:05.570 --> 00:34:08.310
get that most other people
don't get was huge for me, and

00:34:08.310 --> 00:34:09.790
I think was an asset to my application.

00:34:10.805 --> 00:34:11.395
How about you, Sara?

00:34:11.395 --> 00:34:14.895
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I would definitely
second the leadership component.

00:34:14.895 --> 00:34:20.375
I had the opportunity to work in two
very different areas in the Army.

00:34:20.375 --> 00:34:23.415
I led combat missions in Afghanistan.

00:34:24.552 --> 00:34:28.945
And I also worked at the Defense
Language Institute where I led education

00:34:28.945 --> 00:34:33.232
initiatives, really a flagship school for
foreign language education.

00:34:33.232 --> 00:34:36.709
The types of leadership you need in
both scenarios are so different.

00:34:36.709 --> 00:34:41.712
And I think that the military offers
you a lot of opportunities to explore

00:34:41.712 --> 00:34:47.660
your leadership style to figure out how
to motivate different groups of people.

00:34:47.660 --> 00:34:50.850
And we have exposure to
lots of different people.

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:54.672
So the military is kind of this
great geographic representation of

00:34:54.672 --> 00:34:57.686
the United States, and
at least in the US military.

00:34:57.686 --> 00:35:01.344
And we have kind of this global
outward thinking approach.

00:35:01.344 --> 00:35:06.310
And I think those elements really
come out in veteran applications.

00:35:06.310 --> 00:35:08.020
&gt;&gt; All right, we're almost out of time.

00:35:08.020 --> 00:35:12.330
So before we head off, Sara and Venit,
do you have any final words of advice for

00:35:12.330 --> 00:35:14.130
anyone out there who's
thinking about applying for

00:35:14.130 --> 00:35:17.320
a Stanford MBA, especially if
they've served in the military?

00:35:17.320 --> 00:35:18.290
We'll start with Sara.

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:21.430
&gt;&gt; I would say absolutely do it.

00:35:21.430 --> 00:35:24.220
You can't catch a fish if
your hook's not in the water.

00:35:24.220 --> 00:35:24.974
So try.

00:35:24.974 --> 00:35:29.482
I think, one of the things I love to
highlight is the Veterans Club and

00:35:29.482 --> 00:35:31.920
the veterans community here.

00:35:31.920 --> 00:35:33.800
I think we're small but mighty.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:39.385
We have a good representation of the
different services and also international

00:35:39.385 --> 00:35:45.030
vets as well, especially we have some
from Korea and from Israel, especially.

00:35:47.820 --> 00:35:48.673
So definitely apply.

00:35:48.673 --> 00:35:52.460
And what was the first
part of your question?

00:35:52.460 --> 00:35:53.420
&gt;&gt; That was basically it.

00:35:53.420 --> 00:35:55.716
So final words of advice,
if you're applying for a Stanford MBA,

00:35:55.716 --> 00:35:57.200
especially if you served in the military.

00:35:58.490 --> 00:35:59.180
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:35:59.180 --> 00:36:00.250
&gt;&gt; Awesome.

00:36:00.250 --> 00:36:02.885
&gt;&gt; Apply.
&gt;&gt; I would second Sarah's thought.

00:36:02.885 --> 00:36:06.825
I only applied quite frankly
because my wife convinced me to.

00:36:06.825 --> 00:36:08.340
GSB was my number one choice,

00:36:08.340 --> 00:36:10.500
but I thought I'd never
get into the school here.

00:36:10.500 --> 00:36:11.980
And I applied very late,

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:17.200
at the part of the first season and
I'm here now, I'm very grateful for that.

00:36:17.200 --> 00:36:20.930
And the other thing I would say as a
recommendation is really hone your story.

00:36:20.930 --> 00:36:24.550
GSB is really about stories, and if you
don't have your story really tight about

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:28.070
what makes you special and different,
it's going to be hard to get in here.

00:36:28.070 --> 00:36:30.294
&gt;&gt; Totally agree on that point, and for

00:36:30.294 --> 00:36:33.897
me my piece of advice on honing
your story is to start early.

00:36:33.897 --> 00:36:38.858
Ideally, you'll take plenty of time
to really craft those essays in

00:36:38.858 --> 00:36:44.060
particular to give the most authentic
picture of you that you can.

00:36:44.060 --> 00:36:47.760
And don't write the essays
thinking that you have to appear

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:50.465
as this incredibly super-hero type person.

00:36:50.465 --> 00:36:54.648
Be authentic and be you and show what
you've truly done through your stories and

00:36:54.648 --> 00:36:57.905
your experiences that makes
you differentiated and unique.

00:36:57.905 --> 00:37:01.805
And if you focus more on just being true
to yourself and let that come through,

00:37:01.805 --> 00:37:04.986
I think you'll find great success and
hopefully find yourself

00:37:04.986 --> 00:37:08.725
among the next year's crop of applicants
or whenever you come to the GSB.

00:37:09.780 --> 00:37:12.580
All right, thank you so
much to Sarah and Venit.

00:37:12.580 --> 00:37:16.750
If you'd like, there are a tremendous
amount of resources and

00:37:16.750 --> 00:37:19.990
points of contact available for
you on the GSB Veterans Club website.

00:37:19.990 --> 00:37:21.840
Just Google that, it'll come right up.

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:24.443
There is also a contact form out
there if you'd like to try and

00:37:24.443 --> 00:37:26.940
get in touch with any member
of the Veterans Club.

00:37:26.940 --> 00:37:28.970
We're here as a resource to help you, and

00:37:28.970 --> 00:37:31.090
if we don't know the answer
to a particular question,

00:37:31.090 --> 00:37:34.980
we can definitely put you in touch
with someone who does know the answer.

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:37.680
So with that we'd like to say,
thanks so much for joining us today.

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:40.280
It's our absolute pleasure
to speak with you.

00:37:40.280 --> 00:37:45.890
Good luck, and
we hope to see you all soon.

00:37:45.890 --> 00:37:48.610
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:37:48.610 --> 00:37:51.820
[MUSIC]

